{- This file shows the basics of Tikka's variant of Haskell. As with any Haskell program, the entry point is the function "main" which must be
   found in any file run by Tikka. Tikka's "main" function is different however, as it can have any type (which will be output by the program) 
   and does not require the IO () monad or output functions such as "print" (don't worry about these if this is your first time using Haskell).

   Note: files run by Tikka can have any file extension. We deliberately choose not to use '.hs' (here instead we use '.tikka') to differentiate
   from typical Haskell files which begin with the "module" keyword.
-}

main = addFive 2

-- Here we give "addFive" a type signature to say that it is a function which takes a number (a Double, Tikka's number type) and returns a number
-- Type signatures are not required as Tikka infers them automatically from the function itself, but are sometimes useful for learning or just clarity
addFive :: Double -> Double 
addFive x = x + 5

-- addFive can also be written as a "lambda function", which uses the lambda notation "\x ->" to signify an input.
-- addFive and addFiveLambda will be interpreted identically by Tikka
addFiveLambda = \x -> x + 5
